## 5. The GRC Pillar filter system (how it actually works)

This is the most "custom" piece of logic in the project, so it's worth understanding end to
end:

1. **Tagging** — `standards`, `projects`, `artifacts`, `maturitySnapshot.domains`, and each
   `experience` bullet all carry a `pillar` field: `'governance' | 'risk' | 'compliance'`.
2. **`PillarBadge.jsx`** reads that field and renders a small colored tag with the matching
   label — it's purely decorative/informational, no filtering logic of its own.
3. **`PillarFilter.jsx`** is the tab bar ("All Pillars / Governance / Risk / Compliance").
   It's a "dumb" component — it just calls `onSelectPillar(id)` when clicked. It doesn't
   know what "governance" means; whoever uses it (e.g. `Skills.jsx`) decides.
4. **The section component** (e.g. `Skills.jsx`) holds the actual filter state:
   ```js
   const [activePillar, setActivePillar] = useState('all');
   const filteredStandards = useMemo(() => {
     if (activePillar === 'all') return standards;
     return standards.filter((s) => s.pillar === activePillar);
   }, [activePillar]);
   ```
   Then it renders `filteredStandards` instead of the full `standards` array.

**To add pillar filtering to a new section**, copy this same pattern: import `PillarFilter`
and `PillarBadge`, add a `useState('all')`, filter your data array by `.pillar`, and render
`PillarFilter` above your list with an `onSelectPillar={setActivePillar}` handler.

